// The guest's two byte streams.
//
// They live on the stage -- a `pub var` is a global, and the stage is where a
// global is declared -- because two sprites own the two ends of each one. The
// input sprite pushes what the keyboard typed and the core pops it when the
// guest reads a console CSR; the core pushes what the guest printed and the
// terminal drains it once a frame.
//
// Codes rather than characters: the guest writes a byte to `0x10000000` and the
// core has to hand the terminal the same byte, and Scratch cannot make a
// character out of a number. `rv32::data`'s `byte_chars` is the table that
// turns one into the other, and the terminal is the only thing that needs it.

pub var input_buffer: list<num> = [];
pub var output_buffer: list<num> = [];
